Another Claude session sent a message:
<teammate-message teammate_id="clean-altitude" color="blue" summary="Altitude review: 9 findings, mostly a missing clock">
ALTITUDE REVIEW — 9 findings. Not clean. The dominant theme: pace is a clock-dependent value and nothing owns the clock, so both surfaces improvised. `apps/desktop/src/renderer/hooks/useNow/useNow.ts` already exists and is exactly the missing mechanism.

1. {C:\Users\khair\Code Projects\fork-superset-windows-arm64\apps\desktop\src\renderer\routes\_authenticated\_dashboard\components\DashboardSidebar\providers\ClaudeAccountSidebarProvider\ClaudeAccountSidebarProvider.tsx:122-128,185-187 | Provider precomputes a clock-derived value, then needs four coupled query settings to make it re-derive | `refetchInterval` + `refetchIntervalInBackground` + `notifyOnChangeProps:["data","dataUpdatedAt"]` + `roster.dataUpdatedAt` in the memo deps exist only to keep pace ticking. Deeper form: carry raw `fiveResetsAt`/`sevenResetsAt` (2 fields, needed anyway for the fable rule) and let the chip call `usagePaceLevel(..., useNow(60_000))` like the picker already does; `entriesEqual` sheds 3 comparisons and the memo sheds its time dep. Cost: repaint cadence is welded to network cadence — a roster fetch that errors or dedupes leaves colours frozen at the last successful payload. The `notifyOnChangeProps`/`dataUpdatedAt` pairing is a tripwire: remove either and colours silently stop ticking, with no test covering it.}

2. {...\ClaudeAccountPicker\ClaudeAccountPicker.tsx:223 | `const now = Date.now()` captured at render, so an open menu never ticks | Threaded through AccountRow → AccountMetrics → PctSpan/ResetSpan. A countdown reading "4h0m" stays "4h0m" for as long as the submenu is open — a countdown that does not count down. Cost: same missing clock as #1, opposite failure mode; two surfaces now hold two different half-answers to "when does pace refresh", and neither owns it.}

3. {ClaudeAccountIndicator.tsx:18-33 and ClaudeAccountPicker.tsx:80-135 | The three metrics are hand-enumerated at both call sites | `usageSlots()` is three near-identical if-blocks; `AccountMetrics` is three near-identical PctSpan blocks plus two ResetSpans. Both encode the same list, same order, same pct↔resetsAt↔windowMs pairing. Deeper form: one `usageMetrics(account): {key,label,pct,resetsAt,windowMs}[]` in claudeUsagePace that both surfaces map over — components then stop importing the window constants at all. Cost: a fourth window means editing two components, the provider, `entriesEqual`, and the `ClaudeAccountSidebarAccount` interface.}

4. {ClaudeAccountSidebarProvider.tsx:161-163 and ClaudeAccountPicker.tsx:114-116 | "Fable shares the weekly boundary" is duplicated, as prose, at the UI layer | Both pass `account.sevenResetsAt` for the fable metric with a comment explaining why. This is a fact about the Pi payload, and the payload is mapped in exactly one place: packages\host-service\src\claude-accounts\pi-client.ts:217-219. Deeper form: emit `fableResetsAt: account.seven_resets_at` there, or fold it into #3's descriptor. Cost: the rule is enforced only by two comments — if the Pi ever splits the boundary, nothing fails and the app quietly paces Fable against the wrong window.}

5. {apps\desktop\src\renderer\lib\formatResetTime\formatResetTime.ts:16-42 | `formatResetCompact` sits in the shared time lib but is Claude-usage-only | Its siblings are genuinely shared (UsageView + picker); this one has a single caller and takes `windowMs` purely for domain-specific clamping. A general "format a countdown" helper should not know what a usage window is. Deeper form: caller clamps and the formatter formats a plain interval, or the function moves next to claudeUsagePace where the window is already first-class. Cost: formatResetTime.test.ts:9-10 re-declares FIVE_HOUR_WINDOW_MS/WEEKLY_WINDOW_MS locally because it cannot import them from a sidebar util — that is a third copy of the window lengths, in a test, where drift is invisible.}

6. {packages\host-service\src\claude-accounts\profile-manager.ts:111-120,421-433 | Blanked state uses an exception where the same file already has a state union 40 lines above | `readGlobalIdentity()` classifies the machine-wide file into `GlobalIdentity = absent | tray | unmanaged` (types.ts:59-62). The new per-workspace classification throws `BlankedCredentialsError`, caught by `instanceof` at index.ts:1241. The forcing function is that `readProfileCredentials` returns `ManagedCredentials | null` — no room to say "blanked". Deeper form: mirror the neighbour with `ProfileIdentity = absent | managed | blanked`; the existing `null` is already an untyped stand-in for "absent", and the caller's `instanceof` becomes a switch. Cost: two vocabularies for one job in one file — and blanked is not an error, the service logs, warns and returns null to proceed, which is precisely what a state expresses and a throw does not.}

7. {profile-manager.ts:99-107,424 | Two schemas parsed in sequence to classify one file | `blankedCredentialsSchema` restates `trayManagedAccount: z.string().min(1).nullable()` and the passthrough shape, and is only consulted on the other schema's failure path. Deeper form: one discriminating parse — `z.union([managed, blanked])`, or `accessToken: z.string()` with a refinement naming the empty case. Cost: two hand-synced definitions of "this file is ours"; add a required field to managedCredentialsSchema and a blanked file falls through to the generic validation throw again, silently losing the whole feature.}

8. {packages\host-service\src\claude-accounts\index.ts:44,1420,1428 vs 1337-1343 | The cause vocabulary and the clear-lists are parallel manual enumerations | Adding "blanked" required editing SILENT_WORKSPACE_CAUSES plus two new clear sites, while the existing bulk clear at 1337-1343 enumerates four other causes and does not include it. Nothing declares the cause set or which transitions retire which cause. Cost: the bulk clear and the two new clears now disagree about when "blanked" retires; each future cause is another manual sweep of a 1400-line file with no gate catching a missed site.}

9. {C:\Users\khair\Code Projects\mk-skills\statusline\statusline.py:135-149 | The cache is keyed on the access token when the account slug is sitting in the same file | `_creds_token_sha()` answers "is this the same token", which is strictly narrower than the question asked. The file already carries `trayManagedAccount`, written by the host-service, next to the token. Cost: an ordinary token refresh for the *same* account changes the hash, so the cache misses AND `backoff_until`/`backoff_count` are discarded — an extra api.anthropic.com fetch plus a reset 429 backoff on every routine refresh, on a path whose entire design is 429 avoidance. Keying on `trayManagedAccount` (token hash only as fallback when absent) matches the docstring's stated intent. Note: with that fix the `_CONFIG_TAG` filename hash becomes redundant — the slug alone separates both workspaces and in-place swaps, so the two parallel identity keys collapse to one.}

Right-depth things I checked and would not change: `claudeUsagePace` living at `DashboardSidebar/utils/` is the correct highest-shared-parent (provider + picker + indicator all sit under it); `displayPct` clamping so colour bands the number actually on screen; `USAGE_PACE_CLASS` as a lookup rather than branching in JSX; the CONTEXT.md "Pace colour" glossary entry.

One thing I could not verify: CONTEXT.md now claims "The tray, the round display and the app share the same bands." Nothing in this repo implements the tray or round-display bands, so that sentence asserts a cross-system invariant with no shared source and no test. If the bands in claudeUsagePace.ts:40-47 were transcribed from the tray by hand, that is a fourth copy of duplicated knowledge and the doc line is the only thing holding it together.
</teammate-message>

